HST Office: the coat's switch is not stuck at its default when the setting is read too early, and the gtk3
coat CSS goes away when LibreOffice's own high-contrast setting turns high contrast on.

- hst::coat::IsSwitchedOn: an unreadable UiCoat/Enabled (no configuration very early in start-up) gives the
  shipped default (on) without remembering it, so the real setting applies once it can be read (as the
  frame parts' FrameCoat switches already do). Tests can replace the reader (SetSwitchReaderForTesting).
- High contrast on gtk3: the frame's UpdateSettings installed the coat's screen CSS from the GTK theme alone,
  and Window::ImplUpdateGlobalSettings applies Accessibility/HighContrast and SAL_FORCE_HC only afterwards,
  so the coat stayed on in LibreOffice's high contrast. vcl now passes the final global style settings to
  a listener (hst::coat::SetGlobalStyleListener / NotifyGlobalStyle, one call in settings.cxx); the gtk3
  backend registers one and installs or removes its CSS from them, and keeps that final high-contrast mode
  for the frame updates the menu bar and menus make directly (which take high contrast from the GTK theme
  name only). Other backends register none and already read the final settings when they paint.
- CppunitTest_vcl_hst_coat: an unreadable switch is not remembered; the listener hears the high-contrast
  setting on and off; no listener, no call.

